# Credits and third-party licences

This project is an independent rework of an existing MIT-licensed plugin. The
mapping between Xbox HID reports and OrbisPadData in `core/src/translate.c` is
derived from that plugin and reproduces its behaviour deliberately, so that any
difference observed on the console can be attributed to our changes rather than
to the port.

## crucifix86/xbox_controller_plugin

* Source: https://github.com/crucifix86/xbox_controller_plugin
* Licence: MIT
* Used for: the Xbox 360 / Xbox One / Switch HID report layouts
  (`core/include/xcp/report.h`), the button and axis mapping
  (`core/src/translate.c`), the product-id lists (`core/src/report.c`) and the
  `XCP_BTN_SHARE` / `XCP_BTN_PS` bit values, which are that project's guesses
  rather than documented Orbis values.
* Revision studied: see `third_party/REFS`.

## ps4-payload-dev/sdk (ps4-payload-sdk)

* Source: https://github.com/ps4-payload-dev/sdk
* Licence: **GPL-3.0** — the only third-party component here that is not
  permissive.
* Used for: the CRT and link script that make a GoldHEN **payload** loadable (the
  `_start` that calls `main()`, the runtime that resolves Sony imports by name,
  and `elf_x86_64.x`). `./build.sh payload` clones the repository for those sources
  and compiles them with Zig; its build system (make + LLVM) and its sysroot are
  not used. Nothing of it is copied into this repository.
* Consequence worth knowing before shipping a payload: the ELF that comes out
  contains that CRT, so **the payload binary is GPL-3.0** even though the sources
  in this repository stay MIT.
* Revision: see `third_party/REFS`.

## MoHadiShibli/Control4Free and seregonwar/SplashDown

* Sources: https://github.com/MoHadiShibli/Control4Free,
  https://github.com/seregonwar/SplashDown
* Licence: GPL-3.0 (both)
* Used for: **behaviour only, no code.** These are the two projects known to work
  on a real console, and they are where the payload's design facts come from: the
  order `dlopen(libSceMbus)` → `scePadInit` → `scePadSetProcessPrivilege(1)` →
  `AddDevice`, the fact that `AddDevice` returns a status rather than a handle
  (0x803b0006, which this project then patches that one call to neutralise - see
  PROGRESS.md §4.10), that the handle is the low 32 bits of the MBus DeviceId
  announced in `/dev/klog`, the `SCE_MBUS_EVENT_DEVICE_ADDED` / `subType:2` shape
  of that line, and the 120-byte pad sample with a rising timestamp and counter.
  Our implementation of all of it is our own; the licence boundary is the reason
  this paragraph exists.

## vld439/anypad-ps4 (AnyPad PS4)

* Source: https://github.com/vld439/anypad-ps4
* Licence: **GPL-3.0-or-later**
* Used for: **behaviour only, no code.** AnyPad PS4 demonstrates that a payload can
  create a virtual pad on newer firmware without the 11.00 callsite patch by raising
  its credentials to authid `0x3800000000010003` with full capabilities, calling
  `scePadVirtualDeviceAddDevice` directly with device type `1`, and reading the
  `DEVICE_ADDED` event from GoldHEN's klog TCP server on port `3232`. Those API facts
  guided the direct-path, klog and diagnostic work in this project. The implementation
  here is independent; AnyPad is GPL-3.0 and none of its source is copied into this
  MIT-licensed tree.

